.TH IO_DUP 3 "c-utils" "C Utility Functions"
.SH NAME
io_dup \- duplicate a file descriptor or handle
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <io.h>
.PP
.BI "int64 io_dup(int64 " fd ");"

.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR io_dup ()
duplicates an open file descriptor or handle
.I fd
and returns a new descriptor or handle referencing the same open file description, socket, or kernel object.

On POSIX/Unix systems,
.BR io_dup ()
wraps the system
.BR dup (2)
call to allocate the lowest available file descriptor number.

On native Windows platforms,
.BR io_dup ()
uses
.BR DuplicateHandle ()
for the current process with
.B DUPLICATE_SAME_ACCESS
and no inheritance (\fBFALSE\fR).

The newly created descriptor or handle shares file offsets, flags, and lock states with the original. Note that
.BR io_dup ()
duplicates the system descriptor/handle only; it does not automatically register the new descriptor with the
.B io
library event loop. To register the duplicated descriptor for I/O event monitoring, pass it to
.BR io_fd (3).

.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR io_dup ()
returns the newly allocated file descriptor or handle value. On failure, it returns \-1 (or
.B INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE
on Windows) and sets
.B errno
accordingly.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR io_fd (3),
.BR io_close (3),
.BR dup (2)